 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Embroidery tools:\n• embroidery needles (crewel needles): sharp, with a long eye for thick thread; tapestry needles: blunt, for counted-thread work;\n• embroidery thread: stranded cotton (six strands that can be separated), perle cotton, wool, metallic thread;\n• embroidery scissors: small and sharp, to cut threads close to the fabric; dressmaker's shears for fabric;\n• an embroidery hoop (two rings) or frame: keeps the fabric tight;\n• a thimble: protects the finger that pushes the needle;\n• tracing paper, carbon paper or a water-soluble pen: to transfer designs;\n• a needle threader, pins, a tape measure.\nCare: keep needles in a needle case, scissors closed and dry, threads sorted; never use embroidery scissors to cut paper.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why should embroidery scissors never be used to cut paper?",
  "steps": [
   "Embroidery scissors are small and very sharp for cutting threads.",
   "Paper makes the blades blunt quickly."
  ],
  "answer": "Paper blunts the fine blades.",
  "checks": []
 },
